WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.047 --> 00:00:06.735
Odysseus and his men left the Island of the Cyclops, not realizing that Polyphemus had asked his father Poseidon to curse them.

2
00:00:06.736 --> 00:00:14.100
Unlike mortal men, the gods can be quite patient in their revenge, so Poseidon allowed Odysseus and his men to sail where the wind took them.

3
00:00:14.101 --> 00:00:20.139
After several days of sailing they came to a strange island that seemed to be floating on the sea, like a large vessel.

4
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:24.496
The island was inhabited, because there was a large villa with a windswept pavilion.

5
00:00:24.497 --> 00:00:27.488
The craftsmanship of the villa and the pavilion were incredible.

6
00:00:27.489 --> 00:00:33.250
"This place is too strange and too beautiful to be the work of mortal men," said Odysseus to his crew.

7
00:00:33.251 --> 00:00:38.640
"Let us go there cautiously, because we do not want to make the gods or people who live there angry."

8
00:00:38.641 --> 00:00:43.268
His crew agreed, and they drew lots to see who among them would accompany Odysseus.

9
00:00:43.269 --> 00:00:47.507
From his own portion of the treasure, Odysseus chose a gift to bring their host.

10
00:00:47.508 --> 00:00:51.409
It was a broach made of gold, silver, and jewels in the shape of a butterfly.

11
00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:55.711
"In beauty there is nothing finer than this broach," said Odysseus to his men.

12
00:00:55.712 --> 00:01:01.051
"I chose this as a gift for my wife, but a beautiful present is worthless so far from home.

13
00:01:01.052 --> 00:01:06.138
I hope its beauty will inspire the god or goddess who will be our host to hasten our journey."

14
00:01:06.139 --> 00:01:10.337
The men nodded and appreciated the sacrifice that their leader was making for all of them.

15
00:01:10.338 --> 00:01:17.052
Cleanliness is next to godliness, so Odysseus and the men chosen to go with him bathed in the ocean before leaving the ship.

16
00:01:17.053 --> 00:01:20.981
The villa and pavilion were located on a steep mountain that overlooked the beach.

17
00:01:20.982 --> 00:01:26.290
They could tell that the trail up to the villa was seldom traveled because it was overgrown with grass and weeds.

18
00:01:26.291 --> 00:01:31.464
As Odysseus and his men approached the top of the mountain they thought they could hear someone or something whistling.

19
00:01:31.465 --> 00:01:37.683
But when they entered the pavilion there was no one; only the four winds chasing themselves among the columns of the pavilion.

20
00:01:37.684 --> 00:01:41.589
From the villa, Odysseus and his men heard what sounded like a party.

21
00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:44.049
There was shouting, singing, and much laughter.

22
00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:48.021
"I think our host is throwing quite a party," said Odysseus to his men.

23
00:01:48.022 --> 00:01:53.283
"If fate has not doomed us to die here upon this windswept rock, I think we shall enjoy ourselves."

24
00:01:53.284 --> 00:01:56.474
Odysseus and his men eagerly went to the door of the villa.

25
00:01:56.475 --> 00:02:01.101
But before Odysseus could knock, the four winds came rushing past, and blew the door wide open.

26
00:02:01.102 --> 00:02:05.077
"Quiet!" shouted a tall man with windswept silver hair.

27
00:02:05.078 --> 00:02:08.807
"The winds are my pets, and they have brought me news of visitors.

28
00:02:08.808 --> 00:02:11.529
Let us see who has come to the island of the winds."

29
00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:16.994
Odysseus bowed low and his men did likewise, "Great and noble immortal, I am Odysseus.

30
00:02:16.995 --> 00:02:19.479
I have brought you a token of our goodwill."

31
00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:24.613
From beneath his cloak, he took out the wondrous jewel-encrusted butterfly and gave it to the god.

32
00:02:24.614 --> 00:02:28.202
"My men and I are returning from our victorious triumph at Troy."

33
00:02:28.203 --> 00:02:33.731
"Well met, Odysseus. I am Aeolus, the keeper of the winds, and they are my children.

34
00:02:33.732 --> 00:02:40.525
This is indeed a broach of the finest craftmanship; it is, I think, too fine to be worn. I will set it free."

35
00:02:40.526 --> 00:02:45.110
Aeolus blew on the butterfly, and it spread its wings and fluttered around the room.

36
00:02:45.111 --> 00:02:50.340
"Thank you for your gift, and please do not think me rude, but I would like to make a request of you.

37
00:02:50.341 --> 00:02:53.883
I grow weary of the conversation and songs of my children.

38
00:02:53.884 --> 00:02:59.068
If you tell me the story of your triumph, I will give the wayward voyager a most precious gift."

39
00:02:59.069 --> 00:03:03.514
Odysseus and his men gladly retold their tale of battles won and battles lost.

40
00:03:03.515 --> 00:03:17.325
Aeolus and his children were greatly entertained, and Aeolus took Odysseus aside and gave him a large, leather bound sack saying, "Tie this sack to the mast of your ship, for I have captured all the winds except for the west one which will take you home.

41
00:03:17.326 --> 00:03:20.904
The west wind will chase her friends and take your ship home.

42
00:03:20.905 --> 00:03:26.055
Do not open the sack until you are home, or the other winds will drive you away in anger."

43
00:03:26.056 --> 00:03:28.809
Odysseus thanked Aeolus, and did as he said.

44
00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:34.275
He was so happy to be finally on his way home that he stood at the wheel of his ship for four days and four nights.

45
00:03:34.276 --> 00:03:39.070
On the morning of the fifth day he and his men came within sight of Ithaca, and home.

46
00:03:39.071 --> 00:03:42.260
Exhausted, Odysseus fell asleep where he stood.

47
00:03:42.261 --> 00:03:50.595
While Odysseus had been sailing, Poseidon had been whispering to the crew, "Odysseus isn't going to share the treasure that Aeolus gave to him.

48
00:03:50.596 --> 00:03:53.650
Open the bag and divide it fairly among yourselves."

49
00:03:53.651 --> 00:04:03.555
While Odysseus was awake no man dared to open the bag, but once Odysseus was asleep, the men in all the ships stared greedily at the large sack tied to the mast of Odysseus' ship.

50
00:04:03.556 --> 00:04:08.021
One of the men climbed up the mast and unfastened the sack's leather strap.

51
00:04:08.023 --> 00:04:09.585
The roar was deafening.

52
00:04:09.586 --> 00:04:13.894
The three imprisoned winds rushed out of the sack, and Poseidon filled their anger with lies.

53
00:04:13.895 --> 00:04:18.967
Soon the winds were chasing Odysseus and his ships across the sea and straight back to Aeolus' island.

54
00:04:18.968 --> 00:04:25.799
After Aeolus heard the winds' tale, he knew that Odysseus had angered Poseidon and he refused to help Odysseus a second time.

55
00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:31.474
Odysseus and his men left the floating island, dreading Poseidon's anger and the fate that awaited them.

56
00:04:31.475 --> 00:04:34.852
The angry winds continued to plague Odysseus and his men.

57
00:04:34.853 --> 00:04:37.694
The winds drove them to an island inhabited by giants.

58
00:04:37.695 --> 00:04:41.772
Odysseus and his men made for the safety of a harbor between two steep cliffs.

59
00:04:41.773 --> 00:04:45.350
Once they were safely within the harbor the winds left them alone.

60
00:04:45.351 --> 00:04:47.135
But it was a trap.

61
00:04:47.136 --> 00:04:50.414
Poseidon had warned the giants of Odysseus' approach.

62
00:04:50.415 --> 00:04:55.287
From the cliffs, the giants hurled massive boulders of granite at Odysseus and his ships.

63
00:04:55.288 --> 00:04:59.102
Soon the boulders were falling like rain, smashing and sinking his ships.

64
00:04:59.103 --> 00:05:03.388
Odysseus ordered his men to flee, but there were too many boulders falling.

65
00:05:03.389 --> 00:05:07.299
And Odysseus watched as each of his ships sank and his men died.

66
00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.370
Odysseus' ship, however, was curiously protected.

67
00:05:10.371 --> 00:05:16.235
Athena had extended the corner of her great shield to protect Odysseus from the death that was happening all around him.

68
00:05:16.236 --> 00:05:20.171
He and his men escaped, but they cried bitterly for the friends they had lost.

69
00:05:20.172 --> 00:05:26.295
They left the land of the giants and sailed knowing that fate had spared them, but not knowing for how long.

